accessWidener	v1	named
# One line, and it buys the chunk tamper its forty-three pictures.
#
# The header has to be the first line of this file - a comment above it is not a comment, it is a bad
# header, and the build says so clearly. So the explanation comes after it.
#
# ItemProperties.register is how an item gains a property its model can be chosen by, which is how a
# tamper is drawn as the metal it is made of at this version. The method is private until 1.17 and
# public from 1.17 on. Forge patches it public at 1.16.5; Fabric does not, and this is Fabric's own
# way of saying the same thing. The two loaders reach one vanilla method by their own routes, and
# TamperModels, which holds the rule, knows about neither.
#
# Narrow on purpose: one method, named in full, rather than the class. A widened class is a standing
# invitation to reach for the next private thing, and the whole of what this edition needs from here
# is one door.
accessible	method	net/minecraft/client/renderer/item/ItemProperties	register	(Lnet/minecraft/world/item/Item;Lnet/minecraft/resources/ResourceLocation;Lnet/minecraft/client/renderer/item/ItemPropertyFunction;)V
# And one private field, for the same kind of reason.
#
# The game's own set of item colour handlers - the one every mod registers into - is what the wear
# editor tints its previews with, and it is private with no accessor at this version. Forge patches
# one back on and needs nothing here; this is Fabric's way of reaching the same field. ItemTints is
# what both hand it to, and says what happens if neither does.
accessible	field	net/minecraft/client/Minecraft	itemColors	Lnet/minecraft/client/color/item/ItemColors;
